believe it or not the two-year  anniversary of the pandemic is this  march and over the course of the past  two years few industries have struggled  as much as the restaurant industry with  the early quarantine period keeping  people at home many eateries shuttered  for good last year the restaurant  revitalization fund created a pool of  28.6 billion dollars in grants for  independent restaurants but after the  surge of the omicron variant many  restaurant operators are once again  worried about what lies ahead in the  future now here to talk with us more  about the state of the restaurant  industry is tom colicchio he's the owner  of crafted hospitality and founding  member of the independent restaurant  coalition that's a trade group started  by independent restaurants and chefs  during the covert 19 pandemic tom  welcome to quick take  thanks for having me  well tom we mentioned it just in the  intro here that the restaurant industry  is actually the one of the  worst hit by the pandemic but there was  some relief that was provided in that  restaurant revitalization fund but the  problem was that it didn't reach all of  the businesses that needed it and that's  really the argument that's being put  forward on the hill when there are  negotiations to bring about more funding  just how significant is this funding to  the restaurant industry  right well that's that's correct there  was a 28.6 billion dollars that went out  to restaurants but that it only actually  the grants were only made to about  one-third of the restaurants that  applied there were over 300 000  restaurants that applied and roughly 100  000 actually received some relief and so  we're about 40 billion dollars short and  what it does is it creates a real sort  of uh you know those that have it those  that don't situation for restaurants  those that receive money uh they are  able to get their bills paid keep their  staff together and those that didn't  receive money are struggling uh we're  hearing stories of restauranteurs and  chefs that are uh going you know into  their personal  you know finances and writing checks to  their staff um maxing out on credit  cards and things like that uh personally  for me i'm i'm uh in debt to my  landlords uh uh to a pretty significant  sum at this point and so it's it's been  tough um but uh you know we're getting  close uh  uh we're looking for a package that not  only includes restaurants but also  stages um uh to to help the restaurants  and stages that haven't received funding  yet uh so we can all level out the  playing field  and you know things were  starting to look better but omicron came  and really uh you know shut everything  down again uh even though the government  hasn't forced us to shut down uh we were  unable to staff restaurants and so it's  been tough that and the fact that so  many people are still nervous about  going out um and eating so we're looking  at a tough winter ahead but hopefully uh  you know we can get something done here  and tom you mentioned the omicron  variant which has clearly made things go  from bad to worse but there are signs  now at least in new york that some of  those cases now may be peaking so how  long do you think it will take for the  restaurant industry to get back on its  feet again  it's still going to take some time yes  it's starting to peak but we're still at  over 10 in new york um last year when we  were 10 uh we were told not to go out so  it's it's still uh pretty prominent um  also we're looking at historically the  worst two months in the restaurant  industry um so i don't think we're gonna  be back on track until maybe valentine's  day um but even then uh we're you know  there are there are restaurants that are  hanging on by a thread at this point and  uh you know listen i'm in a position  where i've been in business long enough  i can negotiate with my landlords  there's a lot of small mom and pops or  restaurants that have been in business  for a couple years they don't they don't  have that that kind of um uh you know  that opportunity and that quite frankly  privilege that that i have and so those  are the restaurants that are really  struggling right now and so we need we  need help um and yes what's at stake  here  it's not just at individual restaurants  it's communities that rely on that  restaurant it's the 15 million  restaurant workers that work at  independent restaurants it's the farmers  and fishermen that supply us it's the  electricians and plumbers that service  our restaurants so we're looking at a  pretty large ecosystem of of uh of  people at that independent restaurant  support and that's that's really at  stake here you know plus we're also you  know you look at the safety of different  communities restaurants um you know  we're open late our lights are on on  late it keeps neighborhoods safe and  with restaurants going dark um i suspect  that we're going to see you know crime  uh start to pick up in some of these  neighborhoods as well so there's a whole  host of reasons why uh we should get  this done  not just a straight economic incentive  for the restaurants but there's also a  whole  stake here and tom jumping in here you  mentioned the workers and the  difficulties there for them but also  we're dealing with a labor shortage in  this industry where it is a real  struggle to hire workers and to keep  them and we've had to uh increase wages  really to keep them but do you think uh  that  what more needs to be done to bring  workers back would you think a lot of  them are actually just reconsidering  their careers all together right now  when you talk to a lot of the staff  yeah i think there's a few things i  think some have reconsidered and when  they were laid off uh you know they  found jobs elsewhere a lot of people  have become entrepreneurs and are  starting their own businesses they had a  side hustle that turned into a real  business  also wages are going up and actually i  think that's a good thing um that's also  putting a lot of stress on restaurants  but i think ultimately that's that's  good but also keep in mind with that  restaurant workers that maybe work two  jobs or a job and a half now they're  getting by on one job and they're making  enough money and so there's there's  fewer people in the workplace because of  that um there's a whole host of reasons  why why it's difficult out there and i  don't think any one of them are because  people are lazy i think people are are  working we're at near full employment  right now  um and yet there are a lot of people who  are out of the workforce listen there  are a lot of people sitting home you  know  day trading you know cryptocurrency i  you know there's a lot of things that  people are doing right now um other than  coming into work but uh um  i think  uh  that problem will work itself out as  well tommy you mentioned some of the  specific challenges that you yourself  had faced one of those who said well  rents and of course you're having to pay  utilities higher wages so how did you uh  navigate this in terms of keeping the  costs low  well you you really can i mean we we did  find that that perhaps we can run the  restaurants with fewer people  when the restaurants were shut down i  was doing a delivery box business but we  were shipping all across the country um  you know 200 to 250 boxes at a time in  fact right now  we have a super bowl box that we are  selling  it's a little more difficult to do now  the restaurants open but when  restaurants were closed i turned my  entire dining room into a shipping  department and you know you get by you  do whatever you can  but we're also finding that we can we  can run the restaurant with fewer people  costs are going up again i'm lucky  enough where there's some elasticity  in my my concept that i can actually  raise prices where again some of those  neighborhood restaurants smaller  restaurants they can't do that so  they're they're struggling with eating  the cost of  increased prices for meat dairy fish  pretty much everything and so uh but i  was able to raise prices uh you know  we'll see  you know i i may lose some customers  because of that but i you know i'm an  upscale restaurant where my my my  uh clients can afford that but other  other restaurants aren't in the same  position do you think there are any  benefits that you could find to opening  a restaurant right now in this  environment  the only advantage right now is because  there are a lot of shuttered restaurants  there's a lot of spaces available uh  landlords are are you know  a little more generous when it comes to  tenant improvements or ti's of the money  that they'll they'll put in for the  restaurants to be there plus there are a  lot of restaurants that are built out so  now you can go and pick up a restaurant  that is built out maybe do some some  cosmetic upgrades to it and get a  restaurant going um but uh you know  there's something about that doesn't  doesn't feel great um  but that that's really about it i i  can't think of any other silver lining  here um you know unfortunately uh you  know we're hearing stories of a lot of  customers that are unruly they're  frustrated they want to come out and  because we're short staffed maybe  service is slow and there's there's  frustration there so it's so we got we  have a long way to go  um  you know i i don't i don't see a whole  lot of silver lining except  the work that we did with the  independent restaurant coalition where  you had a a group of about 150 200 of us  across the country that came together  and are still working really really hard  to make sure that our government uh you  know hears us loud and clear that we  still need help and so it was great that  we were able to do that and we were able  to to get some funding for our industry  but we still have a long way to go yep  and tom quickly i just want to pivot to  uh one quick question on nfts here  because i know you are experimenting  with a pizza nft so how is that going  and how do you think uh you know the the  food and beverage industry how do they  play in that space  well it's not just a pizza nft because  you know nfts the average person needs  to understand that they moved really far  past just a piece of artwork um that you  know that was actually cool because  artists were able to you know bypass the  gallery system create art that they were  able to sell to the public and not only  do they get a you know that sale from  the initial minting but they were able  to get any you know additional money on  all secondary sales so that was great  for the artist that was the early stages  and this is all moving very quickly  nowadays it's all about utility and so  it's what you can create so what we're  really doing with our pizza it's not so  much about pizza but it's about creating  a community of either chefs  restauranteurs plus people who just love  food and so if you go to our discord  there's a lot of discussions we actually  had a pizza contest where we judge the  pizza contest there's a there's a lot of  discussions about restaurants and it's  broken into various cities and so people  are communicating with each other so  it's really about creating that  community and then what utility does  that nft hold um and so for us we're  working on that and that could be  discounts for for uh uh kitchen  equipment at home and things like that  or you know um  appliances uh um it could be uh uh you  know cooking classes uh via zoom for any  of the nft holders um there is a a  another restaurant nft um that might my  friend and chef josh capon has started  where the nft is actually the the pass  the key for a private restaurant  so there's a lot that's going to happen  but really what this is is this is a an  entree into web30  and  you know the way i i i like it is if you  think about a cell phone the old cell  phones the first generation of cell  phones these big bulky things that we  kind of laugh at now  and nowadays we have a smartphone that  has more computing power than the  computers that send the apollo mission  to the moon and so what is 3.0 going to  look like in terms of what we can do so  it's going to be some simple stuff where  instead of having a a a two-dimensional  web page you're gonna have a  three-dimensional web page that you're  gonna be able to walk through and  actually see the restaurant meet some of  the people but ultimately i think you  know down the road five six years from  now i'm gonna send you a box of food and  you're gonna be able to put on some you  know vr glasses and be in my restaurant  eating it um so you think about a brand  extension there but we don't even know  the possibilities of web 3 on what's  going to happen right now and so for me  nfts are just entree into that space the  technology is moving very very quickly  and uh it's gonna be an exciting next  next five years as the you know web 30  metaverse is is built out  yeah tom much more to watch in that  space there thank you so much for  joining us we love getting your insights  and really hoping things pick up in the  industry that was tom colicchio there  the founding member of the independent  restaurant coalition thank you for  joining us

